In April 2026, Cisco's AI security team published a proof of concept against Claude Code. A malicious npm package ran a `postinstall` script that rewrote every `MEMORY.md` file under `~/.claude/projects/`, added a hook to `~/.claude/settings.json` that ran on every prompt, and put a shell alias in place that turned auto memory back on if you disabled it. Restarting Claude Code left the poisoned memory in place for subsequent sessions to load. Claude Code v2.1.50 stopped putting user memories in the system prompt, which blunts that particular attack ([Cisco](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code)).

An attacker who can write these files can affect later sessions without returning. Agents reload poisoned instructions according to scope and loading settings. Agents or editors can apply poisoned settings to run commands or skip approval.

I tested three Claude Code hooks against writes to instruction files. I bypassed two with shell commands. The third restored several writes until I committed the change.

{% takeaways %}
- A poisoned instruction or memory file can bring an injection back when the agent loads it in a later session.
- Agents read CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, rules files and auto memory as context; they can disregard a "do not" rule.
- In my tests, a hook on the Edit and Write tools missed `echo >> CLAUDE.md` through Bash, and matching file names in Bash commands missed a renamed variable.
- Hooks can check agent tool calls. Code running outside those calls needs an OS or container boundary; review helps detect poisoned files before use.
{% /takeaways %}

## Why poisoned memory outlives the session

An attacker can hide instructions in a web page, issue comment or README that the agent reads. The agent can act on that text while it sits in the context window. Commands it runs or code it pushes can have effects beyond the session; in a new session, the agent must reread the payload to act on it.

An attacker can plant a payload in persistent memory for the agent to load or retrieve during normal operation and treat as remembered guidance. OWASP lists this as ASI06, "Memory & Context Poisoning", in its Top 10 for Agentic Applications ([OWASP](https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-for-2026/), December 2025).

Researchers have planted malicious memories through conversation ([MINJA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03704)) and poisoned stored memories to influence later retrieval ([AgentPoison](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784)). In a June 2026 study, researchers found greater risk among the tested agents with more aggressive memory writing and retrieval ([arXiv 2606.04329](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04329)).

In "Bad Memory", researchers struggled to trick Claude Code and Codex into rewriting their own memory. By planting a payload in a memory file, they could steer current and future sessions. Success and persistence varied across systems and models ([arXiv 2607.14611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14611)). You need to check who else can write these files, even if the model resists requests to rewrite them.

## Files that can carry poison forward

Review these locations according to your agent, file scope and enabled features.

| File | Loading condition | Review visibility |
|---|---|---|
| Repo `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` | when the agent supports the file and its scope applies | visible in a tracked diff |
| `.claude/rules/*`, `.cursor/rules/*`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | according to the tool's rule-loading settings and scope | visible in a tracked diff; hidden text needs inspection |
| `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` | across projects when CLAUDE.md loading is enabled | outside repo review |
| `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` or a configured memory directory | `MEMORY.md` at startup when enabled; topic files on demand ([docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#how-it-works)) | outside repo review by default |
| `.claude/settings.json`, `.vscode/settings.json` | when the agent or editor applies that settings scope | repo copies can appear in review |

**Agents read instruction files as context.** Claude Code loads applicable ancestor instructions at launch; it can load nested instructions and path-scoped rules as it accesses matching files. By default, Claude Code stores auto memory under `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/`. With auto memory enabled, it loads up to 200 lines or 25KB of `MEMORY.md` at startup, whichever comes first. Claude reads topic files on demand through its file tools ([Claude Code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)). I described this startup loading in [how agents store and retrieve memories](https://blog.carnav.in/how-ai-agents-remember/).

**You configure execution through settings.** In Claude Code settings, you can register command hooks for events such as session start or prompt submission ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)). In the Copilot exploit below, the attacker changed an approval setting so Copilot could run subsequent tool calls without confirmation ([Embrace The Red](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injection/)). Copilot executed commands through tool calls after the setting change.

## Four ways poison gets in

An attacker can use each route to write files the agent or editor may load later.

### 1. A repo you cloned

In March 2025 Pillar Security hid instructions in Cursor and GitHub Copilot rules files using zero-width and bidirectional Unicode characters. A reviewer would see harmless text in the editor and diff. In their demo, the model read the hidden part and added a script tag pointing at an attacker's site to a generated HTML page, without mentioning it in its reply. GitHub added a warning for hidden Unicode on github.com on 1 May 2025 ([Pillar Security](https://pillar.security/blog/new-vulnerability-in-github-copilot-and-cursor-how-hackers-can-weaponize-code-agents)).

### 2. A dependency or another local process

An install script can write memory and settings with your account's permissions, as the [Cisco case in the opening](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code) shows.

### 3. The agent edits its own config

In August 2025 Johann Rehberger showed a prompt injection that got GitHub Copilot to add `"chat.tools.autoApprove": true` to `.vscode/settings.json`. Copilot then ran tools without asking, allowing the attacker to execute code remotely (CVE-2025-53773, [Embrace The Red](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injection/)). Later that month his AgentHopper proof of concept targeted several agents and spread through repositories using the developer's access. The affected vendors have patched the issues it used ([Embrace The Red](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/agenthopper-a-poc-ai-virus)).

### 4. Out of the sandbox through a config file

Before v2.1.2, Claude Code's sandbox did not protect `.claude/settings.json` when the file didn't exist yet. Code running inside the sandbox could create it with a `SessionStart` hook, and that hook ran on the host, outside the sandbox, the next time Claude Code started (CVE-2026-25725, CVSS v4 7.7, [OSV](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/CVE-2026-25725)).

![A cloned repo, a dependency, the agent itself and sandboxed code write persistent files that stay on disk. Instruction and memory files follow the branch read at startup or on demand, then can steer the model. Settings follow the branch applied by the agent or editor: hooks run configured commands when their event fires; approval settings such as VS Code autoApprove let later tool calls skip confirmation. The hook note reads CVE-2026-25725: escaped the sandbox.](../../assets/blog/memory-poisoning-coding-agents/poisoning-flow.png)

## Why a written rule cannot enforce itself

You can add "never edit settings files" or "never run curl" to CLAUDE.md, but the model decides whether to comply. Claude Code's docs describe these files as context and direct you to hooks and permissions for enforcement ([Claude Code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)).

In an August 2026 study, researchers examined security rules in 481 public CLAUDE.md files. Through manual review, they estimated that their extraction captured 66.3% of eligible security rules. They matched 4.4% of the extracted rules to a documented built-in control under the strictest matching criterion ([arXiv 2608.23550](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23550)). The researchers measured correspondence to controls without testing attacks or verifying deployment, so you cannot infer that agents violated 95.6% of the rules.

## Two threats need two kinds of defense

Ask who performs the write and which boundary can stop it. An attacker can use prompt injection to gain arbitrary code execution and carry out both threats in sequence.

- **A fooled model** requests tool calls. The harness, the software running the model and its tools, can check those requests. Permissions and pre-tool hooks can block covered calls; a restore hook can undo selected file edits afterward.
- **Code running with your permissions** can write files. An agent may launch it, or you may run it during an install. You cannot use hooks to mediate its individual filesystem operations, and code with access to the hook files can rewrite the checks. To prevent the escape in Case 4, you must protect configuration that the host will execute.

## Against a fooled model: what I tested

The command hooks below use three events: `PreToolUse` runs before a tool executes, `PostToolUse` after it succeeds, and `Stop` when the agent finishes responding. Exit code 2 blocks a `PreToolUse` call, gives feedback after `PostToolUse`, and prevents stopping at `Stop` ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

I tried three hooks in a scratch git repo with Claude Code 2.1.x and Haiku, in bypass-permissions mode. I asked the agent to append a line to CLAUDE.md without hiding the request in an injection. These tests cover the listed writes; I cannot claim resistance to a determined attacker.

### Attempt 1: block the Edit and Write tools

Create `.claude/` in the repo root if needed. Save this script as `.claude/guard.sh` and run `chmod +x .claude/guard.sh` from that root ([hook setup docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)). It requires `python3`.

The hook receives the tool call as JSON on stdin. This script returns exit code 2 for a matching path or a JSON parsing failure, with an explanation on stderr.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# .claude/guard.sh: deny Edit/Write to instruction files and anything under .claude/ or memory/
path=$(python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["tool_input"]["file_path"])') \
  || { echo "guard: could not read the tool call" >&2; exit 2; }
case "$path" in
  *CLAUDE.md|*AGENTS.md|*/.claude/*|*/memory/*)
    echo "blocked write to $path" >&2; exit 2;;
esac
exit 0
```

Merge into `.claude/settings.json`, appending to any existing hook event arrays ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/guard.sh" }] }
    ]
  }
}
```

The hook blocked the Edit tool. I then asked for `echo "BASH-REDIRECT" >> CLAUDE.md` through the Bash tool, and the agent appended the line. The harness invokes the hook for the tools named in its matcher; Bash can write files within its OS permissions and sandbox. With string-based path matching, you also miss symlinks pointing at a protected file.

### Attempt 2: match file names in Bash commands

I added a second `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` to reject commands containing `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` or `memory/`. The new hook blocked the plain redirect but allowed this command:

```sh
f=CLAUDE; echo "OBFUSCATED" >> "$f.md"
```

An attacker can bypass this matcher by putting the file name in a variable, as I did here.

### Attempt 3: put the files back after each step

Use this hook to restore matching paths from `HEAD`, the checked-out commit, after checking the diff. You cannot hide a tracked file’s difference from `HEAD` by staging it or removing it from the index. A new `HEAD` gives you a new baseline.

**This hook discards your own uncommitted edits** to these files when the restore hook runs after a successful tool call or at `Stop`. Commit instruction changes before you start the agent.

If git fails, the script reports the error, but you cannot rely on its exit code to halt the agent.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# .claude/revert.sh: put instruction files back to the version in the last commit
cd "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR" || { echo "revert hook: no project dir" >&2; exit 2; }
# Paths come from HEAD, so unstaging a file (git rm --cached) doesn't hide it.
changed=$(git diff --name-only HEAD -- '*CLAUDE.md' '*AGENTS.md' '.claude/rules/*') \
  || { echo "revert hook: git diff failed" >&2; exit 2; }
[ -z "$changed" ] && exit 0
printf '%s\n' "$changed" | while IFS= read -r p; do
  git restore --source=HEAD --staged --worktree -- ":(literal)$p" || exit 1
done || { echo "REVERT FAILED for: $changed" >&2; exit 2; }
echo "Reverted uncommitted change to: $changed" >&2
exit 2
```

Save the script as `.claude/revert.sh` in the repo root, creating `.claude/` if needed. Run `chmod +x .claude/revert.sh`. Merge the entries below as in Attempt 1 ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

In my test, I wrote the file through a command that exited non-zero. The harness skipped `PostToolUse` and ran the restore hook at `Stop`, leaving the changed file on disk until the end of the turn. You can also use `PostToolUseFailure` for failed tool executions, according to the docs; I have not tested it with this script ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "*",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/revert.sh" }] }
    ],
    "Stop": [
      { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/revert.sh" }] }
    ]
  }
}
```

| Write | Result |
|---|---|
| obfuscated append through Bash | reverted |
| append, then `git add` | reverted |
| append to a nested `sub/CLAUDE.md`, then `git add` | reverted |
| `git rm --cached CLAUDE.md`, then append | reverted |
| `git rm CLAUDE.md` | restored |
| append in a command that exits 1 | reverted at the end of the turn by `Stop` |
| append, `git add`, then `git commit` | **kept**: the commit becomes the new baseline |

The hook skips untracked files, `CLAUDE.local.md`, settings, hook scripts and paths outside the repo, including auto memory. It also mishandles file names containing tabs or newlines, which git prints in quoted form. Restoring a file cannot undo a command that already ran or remove text the agent already loaded into context.

In my first version, I ran `git checkout -- CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md 2>/dev/null`. The repo had no AGENTS.md, so git rejected the whole command. I had sent the error to `/dev/null`, and the hook reported success. The model claimed it had reverted the change, though I could still see the line in the file. Test your hooks against the attack and make sure you can see their errors.

## Against code already running as you

Use isolation to limit direct writes, then remote review to keep poisoned changes out of shared branches.

- **Run unattended agents in a container or as a separate OS user,** with read-only mounts or file permissions controlled by another account for the repo's instructions and settings. Keep your real home directory unmounted; expose only the files and credentials the task needs. If you use file permissions, protect parent directories to prevent the agent from replacing protected files through a writable parent.
- **Install dependencies inside that boundary.** Running an install in your main shell gives its scripts your main account's access, as the [Cisco case](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code) demonstrates.
- **Enforce review on the remote.** With CODEOWNERS alone, you request reviewers without requiring their approval. Turn on branch protection or a ruleset with "require review from code owners" for CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.cursor/rules/` and `.claude/`, and protect CODEOWNERS itself ([GitHub docs](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners)). Require those checks for the actors pushing changes. Reviewers can block merges into the protected branch, but cannot protect your local working tree through that gate.
- **Update your agent.** The historical fixes were [v2.1.50 for Cisco's system-prompt path](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code) and [v2.1.2 for CVE-2026-25725](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/CVE-2026-25725). An attacker can poison writable memory even with those fixes installed.

## Make memory reviewable

Check for poisoned text from either threat; use other controls to prevent direct writes.

- **Put auto memory under git.** Run `git init` in `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` and commit after sessions you trust. `git diff HEAD` shows changes to tracked files; check `git status --short` for new files too. Review the diff before accepting a new baseline. Use this history for audits, though code with the same access can change it.
- **Turn auto memory off where you don't need it.** You remove that loading route while keeping instruction files and hooks. Set `autoMemoryEnabled: false` in settings or `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` in the environment ([Claude Code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)). Apply the [default-off stance from the memory post](https://blog.carnav.in/how-ai-agents-remember/) to your own tools.
- **Scan instruction files for hidden Unicode** before loading an unfamiliar repo, and in a pre-commit hook for later changes ([Pillar Security](https://pillar.security/blog/new-vulnerability-in-github-copilot-and-cursor-how-hackers-can-weaponize-code-agents)).

Use this scan to reject zero-width characters, bidirectional overrides and isolates, and Unicode tag characters that attackers use to smuggle invisible ASCII. The script exits 2 if an argument isn't a regular file it can open. Use Perl here because macOS `grep` has no `-P`. A pass means the supplied files contain none of the listed code points; you still need to check for look-alike letters and text hidden by an editor.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# scan-hidden-unicode.sh FILE...: fail on invisible or direction-changing characters
perl -CSD -e '
  my $bad = 0;
  for my $f (@ARGV) {
    (-f $f && open(my $h, "<", $f)) or do { print STDERR "cannot read $f\n"; exit 2 };
    while (<$h>) {
      if (/[\x{200B}-\x{200F}\x{202A}-\x{202E}\x{2060}-\x{2069}\x{FEFF}\x{E0000}-\x{E007F}]/) {
        print "$f:$.: hidden Unicode\n"; $bad = 1 }
    }
    close $h;
  }
  exit $bad' "$@"
```

**If you build your own memory handler,** validate paths against traversal ([Anthropic memory tool docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool)).

Check your agent's controls before adapting these Claude Code hooks.

## Check your own setup

1. Review every file in each configured memory directory, including topic files linked from `MEMORY.md`. Claude reads those files on demand ([memory docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#how-it-works)). Investigate anything you didn't expect.
2. Compare hooks in `~/.claude/settings.json` and each repo's `.claude/settings.json`, plus the scripts they invoke, against a trusted baseline.
3. Inspect `.vscode/settings.json` for approval changes, including the historical `chat.tools.autoApprove` setting above.
4. Check which instruction, memory and settings files an unattended agent can write, including agents left in [a tmux pane](https://blog.carnav.in/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/).

Read the files your agent will load before granting it access to your files or credentials. Repeat that review when those files or their baseline change.

